entry parent-nuclide
term: Parent Nuclide
related_terms: daughter-nuclide
related_terms: decay
related_terms: radioactivity
related_terms: radionuclide
related_terms: decay-chain
quick_facts: The parent is the nucleus that decays
quick_facts: It is unstable
quick_facts: It changes into the daughter and gives off radiation
quick_facts: It changes by itself
quick_facts: In a decay chain, each daughter becomes the next parent

sources:
    {"organization":"OpenStax","title":"University Physics Volume 3: Radioactive Decay","url":"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/10-3-radioactive-decay"}

definition:
    The nucleus that decays in a radioactive decay. It is unstable. When it changes, it turns into a different nucleus, called the daughter, and gives off radiation. Nobody has to start the change: the parent changes by itself, at a moment nobody can predict for one nucleus.

debunking_snippet:
    “A radioactive atom needs a person to make it decay.” The parent changes by itself. Nobody has to trigger it.
